"**Header:**\n\u2022 Patient: Alex  \n\u2022 Visit type: Telehealth consult  \n\n---\n\n**S - Subjective:**\n\u2022 CC: 4 days of feeling unwell with sore throat, runny nose, evolving dry cough, fatigue  \n\u2022 HPI:\n  \u2022 Onset 4 days ago after exposure to sneezing colleague (possible viral contact from colleague\u2019s children in kindergarten)  \n  \u2022 Sore throat: painful x ~2 days, painful swallowing; now improving  \n  \u2022 Did not inspect throat; no noted white spots/redness  \n  \u2022 Runny nose: initially, now more nasal congestion; nose feels constantly blocked  \n  \u2022 Cough: onset yesterday, dry, non\u2011painful, present throughout day, no diurnal variation  \n  \u2022 Night sweats for last 3\u20134 days; makes him tired/uneasy  \n  \u2022 Fever at onset; now improving with paracetamol and ibuprofen  \n  \u2022 Dyspnea: \u201cwinded\u201d/SOB on exertion; no SOB at rest; not going to gym because of this; able to go to work  \n  \u2022 No chest pain  \n  \u2022 Ear: no pain, no blockage, no discharge  \n  \u2022 Headache: intermittent, generalized  \n  \u2022 No visual disturbance, no photophobia  \n  \u2022 No nausea/vomiting  \n  \u2022 Bowels: working OK  \n  \u2022 Passing urine OK  \n  \u2022 Appetite: eating slightly less than usual; drinking more than usual  \n  \u2022 No rash  \n  \u2022 Myalgia/joint aches started over past couple days  \n\u2022 PMH: Otherwise fit and well; no ongoing treatments  \n\u2022 Meds (OTC): Paracetamol, Ibuprofen (for fever)  \n\u2022 Allergies: No known drug allergies reported  \n\u2022 FH:\n  \u2022 Grandmother: diabetes (likely T2DM per pt)  \n  \u2022 Great\u2011grandfather: eczema  \n\u2022 SH:\n  \u2022 Lives alone  \n  \u2022 Occupation: Accountant; currently increased workload, year\u2011end; mild work stress but mood OK; enjoys job  \n  \u2022 Smoking: Occasional cigars at company events; not regular smoker  \n  \u2022 Alcohol: Occasional, light/social use  \n\n---\n\n**O - Objective:**\n\u2022 Telehealth; no physical exam or vitals documented  \n\n---\n\n**A - Assessment:**\n1. Viral URTI vs early influenza  \n   \u2022 4 days of sore throat (improving), rhinorrhea \u2192 nasal congestion, new dry cough, myalgia, headache, initial fever now settling, night sweats, exertional SOB but stable at rest, functioning at work  \n2. Exertional SOB in context of acute viral illness  \n   \u2022 No chest pain, no rest SOB; appears mild but requires safety\u2011netting  \n\n---\n\n**P - Plan:**\n\u2022 #1 Viral URTI / possible early flu\n  \u2022 Advise rest; recommend 1\u20132 days off work  \n  \u2022 Encourage increased oral fluids to maintain hydration  \n  \u2022 Continue paracetamol and ibuprofen as needed for fever/pain (within usual dosing limits \u2013 implied by counseling)  \n  \u2022 Expect course ~7\u201310 days with gradual improvement  \n  \u2022 If not improving by next week, return for re\u2011assessment  \n\n\u2022 #2 Exertional SOB / red flag monitoring\n  \u2022 Safety\u2011net: seek urgent review/earlier contact if:\n    \u2022 High or persistent fever  \n    \u2022 Worsening breathing or labored respirations  \n    \u2022 Onset of chest pain  \n    \u2022 General deterioration or concern  \n  \u2022 Otherwise monitor symptoms at home as illness runs its course"